{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are systematically too high versus realized prices, especially in downtrends. Apply a downward level shift (roughly 2–6 €/t), increasing with forecast horizon.",
    "Trend underestimation / lag: the model reacts too slowly to sharp sell-offs; it predicts mild declines while realized prices fall faster. Add a negative momentum term when the last 1–2 weeks show sustained declines (e.g., if 10–15d return < 0, steepen the forecast path).",
    "Mean-reversion overshoot: after a local bounce (e.g., a few up days), the model extrapolates recovery too strongly, but realized prices continue to mean-revert downward. Damp positive rebounds by scaling down upward moves (e.g., multiply positive day-to-day forecast changes by ~0.5–0.7 in a falling regime).",
    "Horizon-specific error growth: errors widen materially beyond ~day 10, with the model staying elevated while outcomes drift lower. Use a horizon-dependent correction that ramps more negative with time (e.g., linear drift adjustment becoming more negative out to 30d).",
    "Volatility asymmetry: downside moves are larger than the model anticipates, while upside is overpredicted. Increase downside sensitivity (fatter left tail) by applying larger corrections on forecast days following negative recent returns than after positive returns."
  ]
}
